309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (In the late nineteenth century, a French judge named Gabriel Tarde noticed something strange. Working as an investigating magistrate, he saw that crimes did not appear randomly across the country. Instead, they seemed to spread from person to person, almost like an infection. One town would suddenly see a wave of a particular type of theft; a neighbouring town would follow soon after. Tarde began to wonder whether this pattern applied to human behaviour in general, not just to crime.)
/E (In the late nineteenth century, a French judge named Gabriel Tarde noticed something strange. Working as an investigating magistrate, he saw that crimes did not appear randomly across the country. Instead, they seemed to spread from person to person, almost like an infection. One town would suddenly see a wave of a particular type of theft; a neighbouring town would follow soon after. Tarde began to wonder whether this pattern applied to human behaviour in general, not just to crime.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (His answer was imitation. According to Tarde, nearly everything people do - the words they use, the clothes they wear, the beliefs they hold - is copied, consciously or not, from someone else. A truly original idea is rare. What is common is the endless repetition of that idea once it has appeared, passed from mind to mind the way a wave travels through water after a stone is thrown into it.)
/E (His answer was imitation. According to Tarde, nearly everything people do - the words they use, the clothes they wear, the beliefs they hold - is copied, consciously or not, from someone else. A truly original idea is rare. What is common is the endless repetition of that idea once it has appeared, passed from mind to mind the way a wave travels through water after a stone is thrown into it.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
311 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (Tarde drew an important distinction between two kinds of imitation. The first he called custom: the slow, steady copying of old models, handed down from parents to children, from one generation to the next. Custom changes little and changes slowly. The second he called fashion: the rapid spread of something new, often borrowed from outsiders or from distant, admired groups, cutting across families and traditions. Fashion moves fast precisely because it ignores the boundaries that custom respects.)
/E (Tarde drew an important distinction between two kinds of imitation. The first he called custom: the slow, steady copying of old models, handed down from parents to children, from one generation to the next. Custom changes little and changes slowly. The second he called fashion: the rapid spread of something new, often borrowed from outsiders or from distant, admired groups, cutting across families and traditions. Fashion moves fast precisely because it ignores the boundaries that custom respects.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
312 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (He also observed a pattern in who copies whom. New behaviour, he argued, tends to flow from those seen as superior - whether in wealth, status, or perceived sophistication - down to those seen as inferior, and from cities, where people live close together, out into more isolated regions. The closer two groups are in social distance, Tarde noted, the faster one will imitate the other; it is easier to copy a neighbour than a stranger on the far side of the world.)
/E (He also observed a pattern in who copies whom. New behaviour, he argued, tends to flow from those seen as superior - whether in wealth, status, or perceived sophistication - down to those seen as inferior, and from cities, where people live close together, out into more isolated regions. The closer two groups are in social distance, Tarde noted, the faster one will imitate the other; it is easier to copy a neighbour than a stranger on the far side of the world.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (Crucially, Tarde believed this spreading almost never happens gradually. Instead, it accelerates the way a rumour does: slow at first, then sudden and explosive once enough people have adopted it, before eventually slowing down again as the novelty runs out. He compared this rhythm to the growth curves seen in nature - populations of insects, the branching of rivers - arguing that society, too, obeys something like a natural law of increase.)
/E (Crucially, Tarde believed this spreading almost never happens gradually. Instead, it accelerates the way a rumour does: slow at first, then sudden and explosive once enough people have adopted it, before eventually slowing down again as the novelty runs out. He compared this rhythm to the growth curves seen in nature - populations of insects, the branching of rivers - arguing that society, too, obeys something like a natural law of increase.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (Finally, Tarde made a claim that still unsettles some readers: fashion, if it survives long enough, does not remain fashion forever. It eventually settles down and hardens into a new custom, only to be challenged, one day, by the next wave of imitation. In this way, he suggested, a society is never simply static or simply changing - it is always doing both at once, absorbing the new until it becomes the old.)
/E (Finally, Tarde made a claim that still unsettles some readers: fashion, if it survives long enough, does not remain fashion forever. It eventually settles down and hardens into a new custom, only to be challenged, one day, by the next wave of imitation. In this way, he suggested, a society is never simply static or simply changing - it is always doing both at once, absorbing the new until it becomes the old.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 105 0 R
/T (Tarde's theory did not receive immediate recognition in his own country, where rival schools of sociology dominated academic life. Yet decades later, scholars studying the spread of new technologies, popular culture, and even rumours online would return to his work, seeing in his nineteenth-century observations of judges' courtrooms and country towns an early sketch of ideas that would only become fully relevant in the age of mass communication.)
/E (Tarde's theory did not receive immediate recognition in his own country, where rival schools of sociology dominated academic life. Yet decades later, scholars studying the spread of new technologies, popular culture, and even rumours online would return to his work, seeing in his nineteenth-century observations of judges' courtrooms and country towns an early sketch of ideas that would only become fully relevant in the age of mass communication.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
