354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 107 0 R
/T (THE SECRET LANGUAGE OF THE YOUNG)
/E (THE SECRET LANGUAGE OF THE YOUNG)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Every generation, at some point, invents a way of speaking that leaves the adults around it completely baffled. In recent years, this phenomenon has reached a new level of intensity. Phrases like "6-7," repeated by children and teenagers with no clear meaning at all, have spread through schools and social media at extraordinary speed, prompting confused parents and teachers to search online for an explanation that, frustratingly, does not really exist.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (What makes such slang unusual is that it resists definition on purpose. When adults ask what a phrase means, young people often reply that it simply doesn't mean anything - it's just funny. This response is not evasive by accident; it is, in many cases, the entire point. A word that cannot be explained cannot be fully understood by outsiders, and a joke that only works because grown-ups don't get it becomes, in itself, a kind of private amusement shared among peers.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Linguists and cultural commentators have offered various explanations for why this happens now more than ever. One theory is that today's young people have grown up constantly observed - by parents, by teachers, by algorithms tracking their every click. Inventing a language that adults cannot decode may be a way of reclaiming a small space of privacy in lives that otherwise feel permanently on display. Nonsense, in this sense, becomes a form of quiet rebellion.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (There is also a commercial dimension to the phenomenon. As soon as a phrase becomes popular among children, it is quickly picked up by journalists, marketers, and content creators eager to explain it to a wider audience. This attention, ironically, can make the joke even funnier for those who started it: the more adults try to analyse the trend, the more absurd and impenetrable the language can become, almost as if it were designed to outrun its own explanation.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (Critics sometimes dismiss such trends as evidence of declining attention spans or a general lowering of standards in youth culture, a phenomenon often mockingly labelled "brain rot." Yet defenders point out that inventing shared, ever-changing vocabulary is nothing new; every generation before this one did something similar, even if the words themselves - and the speed at which they spread - were different.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 107 0 R
/E (What is certain is that today's viral phrases will not last. New words already circulate to replace old ones, and by the time most adults have learned what a term means, young people have usually moved on to something else. This constant churn may be exactly what keeps the game going: the moment a phrase becomes fully understood by outsiders, it stops being useful, and a new one takes its place.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
