/Title (Italy remains one of the larger EU economies that has not yet introduced a nationwide deposit return scheme, even though pressure to do so is mounting. A recent study calculated that a properly designed scheme could lift the collection rate of plastic bottles from about seventy-three percent to as high as ninety-four percent, while cutting visible litter along streets and beaches by roughly a third. The same research suggested that shifting the cost of collection from local taxpayers to the producers of drinks and packaging could save Italian municipalities more than seventy million euros every year. Without such a system, campaigners warn, Italy risks missing the European Union's binding target of collecting ninety percent of single-use plastic bottles by 2029, a deadline now less than three years away.)
